<section id="essay-body" class="grid">
	<p id="p1">
		I have so many wishes and so many concerns, taking on this 'public, creative life' for myself,
		that I saw the only way to arrive at a place of truth within my own spirit, was to discuss it
		with myself. Put pen to paper. Lay it all out. The hope was that I could truly have a concrete
		answer to my hesitations. That my head might spin less when I lay it on my pillow at night. In
		the hopes of doing this transparently, I will break the fourth wall, let you in on the secret.
		Maybe you also share these same feelings and dilemmas, within your own existence and
		communication of your creative work in public. Maybe you have never stopped up and thought of it
		this way, wondered or even thought to care, and maybe you don't care, and that's okay too.
		That's your conscience, not mine.
		<br />
		<br />
		<h5 class="fino text-left">Welcome to my castle</h5>
		<h5 id="castle2" class="fino text-left">&nbsp &nbspin the</h5>
		<h5 id="castle3" class="fino text-right">sky</h5>
	</p>
	<div id="img1">
		<img src={img1.src} alt="" />
	</div>
	<p>
		blaatsofie started out as something totally different than the project I am trying to undertake
		now. It started out as a way to distance myself from my real life name, or rather my last name.
		Personally. Because the name I have used most of my life, has been the one I got from my dad. My
		middle name is my moms, but for whatever reason (there is properly a psychoanalytical reason for
		it), my last name has always been the one I identified with the most. And still, it is just not
		the name I wanna be known for. That kind of credit I do not want to give to my dad. He is not a
		part of my life. It's an ego thing. Maybe a feminist thing? But definitely a thing.
	</p>
	<p class="indent">
		The wish was actually for finding a new last name, a name that would only be mine, free from my
		heritage, its obligations and history. Something fully my own, both personally and
		professionally. But with Danish name laws, that was a totally different kind of undertaking.
		(Sofie Blaat was not really in my realm of possibility. Not guaranteed anyway, even if I put in
		the effort to make it happen.) So I tried blaatsofie out on Instagram, and it stuck. I will
		never be Sofie Blaat, but I will be just (blåt) Sofie.
	</p>
	<div class="grid" id="img3">
		<img src={img2.src} alt="" />
		<img src={img3.src} alt="" />
		<img src={img4.src} alt="" />
	</div>
	<h5 class="bold script">and then came the dream of a brand</h5>
	<p id="p2">
		blaatsofie (<b>the brand</b>) was an innocent idea on its own, initially. A way I knew how to
		sell myself as a creative person, communicate who I am and what I have to offer in a creative
		role. An artist statement, so to speak, in an easy, pretty package, ready for the uncomplicated
		consumption of my peers, future collaborators and you, dear reader. But as with most innocent
		ideas, they grow, they marinate and steep, and you end up with a multifaceted concept, and it's
		not so innocent anymore.
	</p>
	<div id="img24">
		<img src={img24.src} alt="" />
	</div>
	<p id="brand" class="italic">
		Because what is a <em>brand</em>? How do they even work? And what does the
		<em>personal brand</em> have to offer (me)?
	</p>
	<p>
		Typically a brand is built upon a company's values and history. That is what makes them
		relatable, trustworthy, desirable or even envious, and the key to their longevity is for them to
		stay recognizable and consistent. The keyword here is stay. Companies don't change, unless the
		humans inside the organisation decide to change up their marketing and communication, and
		therefore the brand. Otherwise, the company is stagnant, a pillar beside all the others, set in
		stone.
	</p>
	<p class="indent">
		But brands are not only used to target customers by differentiating themselves from the
		competition, but also to signal to the company's collaborators or its own employees, what it is
		that they stand for, and are working towards.
	</p>
	<p id="p3" class="margin-top">
		Social media changed all of this, like every other aspect of how we now live and lead our lives.
		Now everyone can not only be a brand, like Tom Peters urged everyone to in the text <a
			href="https://www.google.com/url?sa=t&source=web&rct=j&opi=89978449&url=https://personalimpact.ca/wp-content/uploads/2015/08/thebrandcalledyou.pdf&ved=2ahUKEwimxsqD8fSSAxXWFBAIHXI-FbsQFnoECBYQAQ&usg=AOvVaw014_o7jG4HneZ5ndCkpSVe"
			>The Brand called you</a
		> in 1997, but also be their own boss while doing it. Through the marketplace of social media, everyone
		now has the tools and power to promote and market themselves, (by the power of the algorithm). You
		can now, not only be recognised, but hopefully, successful, rich and famous.
	</p>
	<p id="p4" class="indent">
		And why does the personal brand work on the consumer? As Tom Peter tells us,
	</p>
	<p class="sans-serif quote end">
		“Anyone can have a website. And today, because anyone can ... anyone does! So how do you know
		which sites are worth visiting, which sites to bookmark, which sites are worth going to more
		than once? The answer: branding. The sites you go back to are the sites you trust. They're the
		sites where the brand name tells you that the visit will be worth your time -- again and again.
		The brand is a promise of the value you'll receive.”
	</p>
	<p>
		And with platforms like Instagram, with around <a
			href="https://www.demandsage.com/instagram-statistics/">2 billion active monthly users</a
		>, this promise is the exact driving power the algorithm utilises. If you keep your promise of
		delivering value to your consumers, they will reward you with showing your work to even more
		people. But if the value you are offering doesn't match what the algorithm prioritises or deems
		worthy of consumption, you get punished, shadowbanned or worse. Keep posting and keep censoring
		yourself, selling stuff in your tiktok shop, making your shein hauls, talking violently about
		women, advocating for genocide, creating ai slop, promoting music with a cute dance, <strong
			>and you will be a star.</strong
		>
	</p>

	<div id="img5">
		<img src={img5.src} alt="" />
	</div>
	<p id="p5">
		What started the excitement and desire for this personal brand of mine, was not the content
		creators, influencers and tiktok dancers, it was the pop stars, the icons and idols. Mainly,
		Charli XCX. Because <a
			href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=_lTpQC9VurE&list=PL-2HG0C5jJQEcsK5zkdNWRJ4Cuee3e63-"
			>brat</a
		> was marketing perfection, a moment, a movement, a summer where we all engrossed ourselves in her
		world of boiler room dj sets, messy partying, raw vulnerability and confrontation with the complexities
		of female friendships, of human existence. The visuals stood clear, and the message even clearer.
		And I was in love (I fucking <em>love</em> the intersection of pop- and subculture). I wanted to make
		myself into an artist with a brand, just like her. Use my dj career as the center, to mold a whole
		world around, with a sharp visual identity, a clear marketing strategy and a corner of the internet
		as my own little sandbox. I wanted to do this to show other artists what I could create with them,
		how I could help bring their creativity and art to more people, by building worlds, with feeling,
		through a honed creative direction, within visual communication and marketing. I never wanted to be
		an artist in my own name (like Charli XCX), I only wanted to make myself the guinea pig.
	</p>
	<p class="indent">
		Creator @nicky.reardon on Instagram said something very clever <a
			href="https://www.instagram.com/reel/DNUKf9wBj4a/?igsh=MXZ2b3hjMXBzZjBnMw=="
			>in an Instagram reel</a
		>, that really made it clear how effective <i>brat</i> and Charli's brand is, and why. He argues that
		the new pop girls have such effective brands, because their success relies on three corroborating
		elements. A strong ethos (what values does the artist have, what do they stand for and what is their
		purpose), a strong personality (who are they and who are their fans) and last, and for me the most
		central part of what makes an artist stand out in this place in time, the artistry (their visual and
		cultural presentation of their work, the world they are building, and how believable said world is,
		what new perspective do they bring to the table, <strong> what do they have to say</strong>).
	</p>
	<p id="p6" class="indent">
		All of the three elements also have to speak to each other. If they start to disconnect, the
		artist's brand falls apart, and with it, their public favor, and what they are trying to sell to
		said public (be it music, a tour, a movie, skincare ect.) is deemed less attractive or worthy of
		consumption. We are trained, as consumers, to look for these cracks, not only in the brands of
		our idols, but everywhere, be it in politics or your local supermarket ail. In the age of
		hyperpolitics, every brand gets more and more fragile, even our personal ones. So with this
		fragility, comes an even bigger need to please the 'public', and slowly but surely, you are no
		longer human, you are a brand, a company, and if your values change, or are no longer
		consistent, reliable, predictable, you will fall out of favor, your value drops, and so they
		will go to the competition for another fix. You are a McDonalds, not a person (for better and
		for worse), and this is what I fear happening to <em>me</em>.
	</p>
	<div id="img6">
		<img src={img6.src} alt="" />
	</div>
	<p>
		But the world has also forced us creatives to strive to be in a mega industry. That, to succeed
		as an artist, you have to (in some way) give away your reigns, to a bigger, better corporation.
		There are rules to this game, and they know the shortcuts, and so, many have won, many have
		lost, and a lot have been exploited in the pursuit of this successful, creative life. But going
		into this business, I believe the artist needs to figure out what kind of success they strive
		for. Is it money and fame, or is it living a long sustainable, creative life, comfortably?
		Because this industry wants you to go for the first one, unless you find the right people to be
		around and do it with. I dream of a comfortable creative life. I am choosing the hard,
		'unconventional' way.
	</p>
	<p id="p7" class="indent">
		I stumbled upon <a href="https://www.instagram.com/p/DPsmnHTjEem/">this quote</a> by philosopher Joel
		Uili.
	</p>
	<p class="sans-serif quote end">
		“They'll tell you that the arts and humanities aren't practical and then read poetry at funerals
		and weddings, cry over films and search for meaning in ancient philosophy. Surviving is one type
		of practicality, knowing why we bother is another.”
	</p>
	<p id="p8">
		And in this lies the truth about this calling, this vocation towards a life filled with
		creation, exploration and endless curiosity that I have dreamed of my entire life. I want to
		know why we bother, I don't want to just survive.
	</p>
	<p class="indent">
		Fame and money has nothing to do with this innate craving for creativity within me. The world
		around us is concerned with it, because the demand for the arts is there. But we as people crave
		art and creativity, we consume so much of it, because it is what makes us feel alive. It speaks
		to something inside us, that nothing else can touch. Our humanity. It gets us through all the
		tough, hard stuff, it keeps us going, makes us survive. Even when we don't want to. Exploited,
		hurt, lost and lonely. So the demand is there, they just want to make millions on it, like they
		do with the food we consume, or the rented homes we sleep in. It is not supply = demand, it is
		exploitation. It is <u>capitalism</u>.
	</p>
	<div id="img7" class="grid">
		<img src={img7.src} alt="" />
		<img src={img8.src} alt="" />
	</div>
	<p>
		In wanting this comfortable creative life, there also lies a real fear in having to, not just
		settle, but to constantly fight a world that just wants your labour. There is such a big part of
		me that is so scared of being forgotten as a human. Not in the ego way, remember me for my
		legacy, but that people, my peers and future bosses, consumers and admirers, will forget my
		humanity in the hopes for profit and further consumerism. Being torn apart, exploited and
		discarded. I am so deeply scared of this reality, because we see it on our phones, day in and
		day out. Not only in the creative industry, but everywhere.
	</p>
	<p class="indent">
		By making myself into a brand, I (myself) will therefore take away my own humanity. I will make
		myself into an object, a number, a non human made for the exploitation of my labor and
		purchasing power. If I don't perform within the confinements of the capitalist system, I will
		(probably) never succeed. And by expecting me to perform as a corporation, a company, I will
		ultimately fail, again, because of my humanity. With human values, like feelings and empathy,
		and human conditions, like growth, change and a body that needs rest, I will never be
		consistent, never perfect, never immortal. But do they even see my humanity now?
	</p>
	<p class="indent">
		The goal was never for me to sell anything, it was for me to create and find people to do it
		with. Help other creatives get their work in front of even more people, so we all can be seen,
		inspire emotions, ideas, critique and change, but also have food on the table, because if we
		don't sell something (our art and our labor), we will be homeless.
	</p>
	<div id="img9">
		<img src={img9.src} alt="" />
	</div>
	<p id="p9">
		I think my biggest issue is with capitalism as a system, not the industry per say. It is just
		one arm of this monster that I (we) will continue to crash into. The biggest reason why I am not
		quoting Marx in this piece, is mainly because of the way the goal of communism has been used as
		a shield for dictatorship, oppression and death more than once. But to be frank, so has
		capitalism, with its empires and colonisation, with patriarchy, racism, 1st vs. 3rd world, the
		west vs. the rest. But I also think that I didn't want to quote Marx, because I didn't really
		feel that his world view fit the world we have now. He thought his thoughts in the 19th century.
		I am now writing this in 2026, almost 200 years later. The world has changed.
	</p>
	<p id="p10" class="indent">
		But in <a
			href="https://strandbergpublishing.dk/boger/taenk-os-om-alternativer-til-lynetteholmen/"
			>a book about Lynetteholm</a
		> (about a wonderfully awful project in the Copenhagen Harbour), a contributing author of the book,
		sociologist Nikolaj Schultz, mentioned an ecological class fight. Where the ecological class fight
		is about fighting for a stop to the big consumption and production of stuff, and the destruction of
		nature, Marx' fight is, in simplified terms, between the worker and the employer. Now the workers
		fight against the production as a whole, and this I felt rang more true to my understanding of the
		world. I may be fighting my employer, the owner of the means of production (the tech companies, the
		music industry, the funds), but I am also fighting for a more just world, where I don't have to work
		(as much), where human qualities, well-being, connection and peace, is at the center. Not productivity,
		consumption, exploitation, in the never ending spiral we find ourselves in now.
	</p>
	<p id="p11" class="indent">
		But in there the dilemma also lies, because I know that capitalism and the postmodern society we
		live in, is exploiting me (my ego) through individualism. And by branding myself as a unique
		person, be it to stand out to other creatives or to the world as a whole, I will inevitably make
		a gap around me that only benefits the people in power, and not me, (and if this is not the
		workers' fight's problem in a nutshell, I don't know what is). I would love to be acknowledged,
		seen, heard, praised and to be spoken well about. But by leaning too heavily into my own ego, I
		put myself in a worse position in the long run. Joan Westenberg makes a brilliant point to this,
		in her essay <a href="https://joanwestenberg.medium.com/how-to-quit-capitalism-5044a7290684"
			>How to quit Capitalism</a
		>, which I will need to bring with me on this journey.
	</p>
	<p class="sans-serif quote">
		"The first layer to peel back is the assumption that greed and selfishness are innate human
		traits that any system must accommodate. In reality, people naturally demonstrate cooperation,
		compassion, and social responsibility — capitalist competition is what brings out
		individualistic instincts. Our altruistic nature surfaces when we design systems that appeal to
		humanity's ethical core rather than its transactional periphery."
	</p>
	<p id="p12">
		But it is also hard to stay critical, and not fall into the ease of just living life as it is,
		and not fight against it everyday. Like is it even possible to opt out of our personal brands,
		when we use them to get the jobs we would like to have, the loan in the bank we are applying for
		or the people we would like to date?
	</p>
	<div id="img10">
		<img src={img10.src} alt="" />
	</div>
	<p>
		The deeper I dig, the more frustrated and aggravated I become, because all of these daily
		dilemmas live inside me (and a lot of my peers) on a daily basis, but we each have to
		compartmentalize, to make the days flow, and not lose all meaning. I see this as a strength
		within me, that I am critical and strife for change, but it is also difficult to carry wherever
		I go.
	</p>

	<p class="indent">
		Naomi Klein also describes this moral dilemma in her book, <a
			href="https://naomiklein.org/doppelganger/">Doppelganger</a
		>.
	</p>
	<p class="quote sans-serif end">
		“I wanted it both ways… To be the only clean one in a dirty business. And isn't that what so
		many of us want as we try to win the game of personal branding - or at least not get slain by
		it? We carefully cultivate online personas - doubles of our “real” selves - that have just the
		right balance of sincerity and world-weariness… We go on social media to juice our numbers,
		while complaining about how much we hate the 'hell sites'.”
	</p>
	<p>
		But can you ever win in a system that is not meant to support you? There is no ethical way of
		doing this, no way to keep my hands clean, when capitalism has it's claws in all of us.
	</p>
	<p class="indent">
		In <a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=GVrT9Q-T8tE">In defense of being online</a>, Mickey
		Galvin references a great observation by Cal Newport.
	</p>
	<p class="quote sans-serif end">
		“People don't succumb to screens because they're lazy, but instead because billions of dollars
		have been invested to make this outcome inevitable.”
	</p>
	<p>
		I also feel this way about the personal brand, as the natural progression of the individualist
		society. It is inherent in the development of social media platforms, it is all connected, so
		how could <i>I</i> take it apart? But also, the people I would like to connect with, work for, be
		inspired by, are also to be found here, within these hellish artificial walls, called social media.
		So it would feel like a death to give that up. I don't think I have the privilege to do that, if I
		want this career. Adam Aleksic also makes a sharp observation about 'No Phone People' (as he calls
		them) as an upper-class status symbol, in his essay <a
			href="https://etymology.substack.com/p/stay-on-your-phone">Stay on your phone</a
		>.
	</p>
	<p class="quote sans-serif end">
		“For one, it's the equivalent of sticking your head in the sand and pretending like the
		algorithm doesn't exist. Whether you like it or not, our culture is still being shaped by these
		platforms, and they won't go away by themselves. All of our music and fashion aesthetics are
		either defined by or against the algorithm, which means that even the 'countercultural' tastes
		of the No Phone People are necessarily influenced by it. Engaging with algorithmic media—in a
		limited, deliberate manner—is thus important to understanding your experience in society as a
		whole. Not engaging, meanwhile, makes you vulnerable to being blindsided by sudden social or
		political shifts… If you ignore those, you lose touch with reality as most people experience
		it.”
	</p>
	<p id="creative-direction" class="script">
		if i want a job in creative direction, marketing, promotion of art and the people who make it, i
		think i need to swallow this dirtiness i feel.
	</p>
	<div id="img11">
		<img src={img11.src} alt="" />
	</div>
	<p>
		I have dreamed up this text, as a way to give myself permission to dream of a creative life, but
		also explore how I can make this life for myself, without compromise. Compromise in (my)
		thought, moral, ethics, ideas and ideals. I am a dreamer at my core. I will never willingly fall
		to my knees at the altar of the ordinary, the status quo, the powers which hurt, exploit and
		kill. I have never wanted to be complicit, turn the non seeing eye, be silent. I am a believer
		in justice, a better world, a revolution. But as mentioned in <a
			href="https://www.threadings.io/there-is-no-revolution-without-madness-2/"
			>There is no revolution without madness</a
		>, an essay by Threadings,
	</p>
	<p class="quote sans-serif end">“Within world-making, the ends are the means”,</p>
	<p>
		so how will I get to this beautiful place in the future, without leaving more dirt than I take
		away? I know I have to be realistic, but the autistic in me wants this to be black and white.
		Either I succumb to the people in power, or I step away. If I dip only a toe in, I am still
		complicit. But maybe that's the curse of being anti-capitalist? That I will never find real
		peace with it, while the system still stands.
	</p>
	<p id="p13" class="indent">
		But within world-making, there are so many alleys and avenues you can focus your time on. A
		fellow activist friend of mine gave me some really sound advice a few years back, I still carry
		with me. Because the truth is, a lot of activists, dreamers and revolutionaries, burn out.
		Because we want to change the (whole) world, but the world does NOT want to change. She said
		that I should choose a fight, a specific course, to focus my energy on. So I chose the fight for
		a free, independent Palestine and she chose the fight for climate change.
	</p>
	<p class="indent">
		And we still fight, we follow the news, we go to protests, we boycott and divest, we cry and we
		cheer in this long fight for justice. I am able to sustain this, a bit more, because I know I am
		fighting for something important, and I also know that other people are fighting in their
		corners. We are not alone. So I am choosing Palestine, and then my big fight against the
		exploitative capitalist system will have to wait, <strong>for now</strong>. It doesn't mean that
		I am looking away.
	</p>
	<di id="img12">
		<img src={img12.src} alt="" />
	</di>
	<p id="p14">
		Through writing this I have changed my position, my feelings on the subject, <em>a lot</em>. And
		in one way, what I set out to do was a success. I wrote an essay. I really looked at how I could
		participate in this race, with algorithms, personal brands and art for money, without losing <b
			>me</b
		>. And although I did not find a definitive answer to the question of how to promote myself in a
		way that I did not find exploitative, in some way or another, I did find some kind of <em
			>peace</em
		>.
	</p>
	<p id="p15" class="indent">
		Because now I am warming up to the idea that I have to use social media to some extent, to put
		myself and my work in front of people. I will have to deconstruct what a personal brand is, <em
			>to me</em
		>, like I have done with terms like success, gender and sexuality. I want an online CV on social
		media, because that is what is the norm within the industry right now, but I am also working on
		an independent website (you are reading this essay on it right now), and I also want to do more
		physical work.
	</p>
	<p id="less-digital">
		(I actually just put a post-it on my wall with the words 'less digital, more physical')
	</p>
	<p id="p16" class="indent">
		I still hate the algorithm, I hate that I am exploiting myself, that they probably will train
		their AI using my work, my blood, sweat and tears, and not compensate me. But I am a human after
		all, I crave to create and connect, always have, always will, so why would I stop, why would I
		stand in my own way. I would rather earn money doing what I love, for passionate people like
		myself, than work an (ungrateful) hospitality job, hate myself, and never let the world know,
		just how crazy and cool, me and my ideas are.
	</p>
	<div id="img13">
		<img src={img13.src} alt="" />
	</div>
	<p>
		I will leave you with an observation Audre Lorde makes in <a
			href="https://www.penguin.co.uk/books/308596/the-masters-tools-will-never-dismantle-the-masters-house-by-lorde-audre/9780241339725"
			>The Master's tools will never dismantle the master's house</a
		>.
	</p>
	<p class="quote sans-serif">
		“For the master's tools will never dismantle the master's house. They may allow us temporarily
		to beat him at his own game, but they will never enable us to bring about genuine change.”
	</p>
	<p id="p17">
		By her words, it sounds like participating in the creative industry, in social media, in
		capitalism as a whole, we would never be able to change the world. But Lorde's words also
		inspire me to think outside the box, to dream of a better world, instead of making the best of
		what is offered at the moment. I dream of creative collectives, of living in community and
		peace, about creating and sharing and being vulnerable with other humans, about carrying the
		financial burden and the insecurity a creative life can contain, with other creatives, sharing
		our strengths and leaning on each other, so everyone can live safely, without giving up their
		creative dreams. And with these dreams in mind, my own brand seems like a shallow but easy way
		to go forward, faster. There is no ethical consumption under capitalism. I can't have both, but
		maybe I can have one, until the other becomes a reality? Because across the world, they try to
		censor and defund art, but none of us would be here today without it. Both fundamentally because
		art inherently <em>IS</em> political, but also emotionally, because it is what gets us through hardships
		and is what memories are made of, what keeps us remembering. Why would I not want to help promote
		art, creating worlds of feeling and emotion?!
	</p>
	<div id="img14" class="grid">
		<img src={img14.src} alt="" />
		<img src={img15.src} alt="" />
	</div>

	<p id="p18">
		Something clicked for me, when I read the essay <a
			href="https://substack.com/@freyaindia/p-186015683">You have to be human</a
		> by Freya India. She argues why we need to learn into our humanity, now more than ever.
	</p>
	<p class="quote sans-serif">
		“So write, think, live. Get hurt and rejected and feel it in your gut and sort of like that
		horrible feeling because at least you're alive, you're here. I realize now that I want to be
		real. I don't want to be harmless, I want to be human, I want to say things that bother people
		and move people and confuse people; I want to start sentences that can't be autocompleted
		because even I don't know where they're going. I want to learn and offend and regret and grow. I
		want to be interesting, irritating, irreplaceable. I want to get things wrong and apologize and
		sometimes I want my opinions to be contradictory and incomplete because I am feeling my way
		through this world and I am not a machine with all the answers. I want to try and be seen
		trying, to be a person you can't perfectly map out and make sense of, what good am I otherwise,
		what am I otherwise.”
	</p>
	<p id="p19">
		Because how could I come to a steadfast conclusion in this piece, if I still have more years
		left to live. Our reality moves with lightspeed (literally), so how could I stand still, with my
		morality intact (and isn't morality subjective anyway)? But to use my humanity radically, like
		joy and love can be radical, wouldn't I fight the system. Because they are still insisting on AI
		taking over the creation of art, before they want to let it replace child labour. My humanity
		would contradict this. By insisting on being human, capitalism will suffer, <em
			>dismantling the master's house</em
		>, and maybe we will see another world.
	</p>
	<p class="quote sans-serif">
		"No, painting is not made to decorate apartments. It's an offensive and defensive weapon against
		the enemy."
	</p>
	<p id="p20">
		<a
			href="https://www.theguardian.com/artanddesign/2010/may/08/pablo-picasso-politics-exhibition-tate"
			>(Picasso)</a
		>
	</p>
	<p id="hope" class="script">at least what i see now,</p>
	<p id="hope2" class="script">is hope</p>
	<div id="img17">
		<img src={img17.src} alt="" />
	</div>
	<div id="img16" class="grid">
		<img src={img16.src} alt="" />
		<img src={img18.src} alt="" />
	</div>
	<p id="p21">
		To circle back to where we started. The intention behind blaatsofie was originally a way to
		distinguish myself from my family, and the heavy legacy that comes with a family name.
		blaatsofie was a libaration from that, from expectations, from damage, pressure, negativity and
		the destruction of self. From living a 'small' life.
	</p>
	<p id="p22" class="margin-top">
		I still dream of a creative life, in the light. Not necessarily the light of fame, but the light
		of being seen, heard and trusted, to expand and grow and connect, because no growth is done in
		darkness. blaatsofie will be my professional self, a part of me, <strong>but still me.</strong> It
		will not be static, predictable and clean cut, but trustworthy, authentic and real, with no sign of
		productivity as the goal. I will not be surprised if I change my mind about tech, social media, algorithms,
		marketing, profit and the creative industry itself, compared with the thoughts and conclusions I share
		with you today. But it will be because of curiosity, growth and experience, because that is what life
		is. I will promise you one thing though, as I go on this journey as blaatsofie as my public, professional
		vessel.
	</p>
	<p id="statement" class="margin-top">
		It will be human to its core, with all its flaws and whimsy.
		<br />
		I guess this is my artist statement.
	</p>
	<p>
		<br />blaatsofie (2026)
	</p>

